### 1.Problem Interpretation

Th objective of this project is to determine a valid order for complting the courses required in the ComputerScience undergaduate program. Many courses require students tocomplete other courses irst, creating prerequisiterelaionships

We represent the couse requirements using a directed graph.Each course is a vertex, and each prerequisite relationshi is a directed edge. Fo examle, if CS 1411 is equired before CS 1412,thegrah contains an edge from CS 1411 to CS1412

The goal is to apply a topological sorting algorithm based on DepthFirst Search (DFS) to produce acourse ordering in which all prerequisites are satified

The supplied dataset consists of 29 courses and 30 prereqisite relationships.
### 2.Methodology

The program is implemented in Java using two classes: CourseGraph and Main.

Graph Representation

The CourseGraph class represents the directed graph usin an adjacency lit implemented with a LinkedHashMap. Each course is associated with a list of courses that depend on it.

This representation allows efficient traversal and supports courses with multiple prerequisites.

DepthFirst Search

DFS is used to explore each course and its outgoing prerequisite relationships.

Each vertex has one of three states:

- Unvisited: The course has not yet been explored.
- Visiting: The course is currently being explored.
- Finished: The course and its descendants havebeen processed.

When DFS finishes exploring a vertex, it adds that vertex to a finishing-order list.

After all vertices have been processed, the list is reversed to generate the final topological ordering.

Cycle Detection

The algorithm also detects cycles. A cycle occurs when a course depends directly or indirectly on itself, making a valid topologica ordering impossible.
If DFS encounters a vertex that is already in the visiting state, the program throws an exception indicating that a cycle exists.

Verification

The program verifies that the final ordering contains every couse exactly once and that every rerequisite appears before the course tat requires it.

Time Complexity
The algorithm has a time complexity of O(V + E), whre V is the number of courses and E is the number of prerequisite relationships.

